program AgentsExe;

// ONE VERTEX OF A BAKED MESH: where it is and which way its surface faces. `MeshAtlas` kept these as
// six parallel float arrays -- `px/py/pz` and `nx/ny/nz` -- and every reader of them reads all six
// for one vertex and writes them straight into an interleaved GL buffer. Six columns feeding one
// row, which is six cache streams doing the work of one.
//
// ---- SIX FLOATS AND NOT TWO `vec3`, WHICH THE TYPE MAP DREW ----
//
// A `vec3` is `<3 x float>` and an array of them strides by SIXTEEN bytes, not twelve: LLVM aligns
// the vector to its next power of two. `Vertex { vec3 at; vec3 normal; }` is therefore 32 bytes
// where six floats are 24, and the biggest atlas in this game is 6.5 million triangles -- nineteen
// and a half million vertices, so the padding alone is a hundred and sixty megabytes to hold
// nothing. The lanes buy nothing here either: nothing does vector arithmetic on a baked vertex, it
// is read once per draw and written to a buffer.
//
// ---- AND NO COLOUR, WHICH THE MAP ALSO DREW ----
//
// The atlas's `cr/cg/cb` are PER TRIANGLE, not per vertex: length `totalTris` against this array's
// `3 * totalTris`. Folding them in would store every colour three times and quietly change what the
// data means. They stay where they are, and the third of a vertex the map gave away is not one.
public bundle Agents {
    public namespace Gfx {

        public struct Vertex {
            public mutable float x;
            public mutable float y;
            public mutable float z;
            public mutable float nx;
            public mutable float ny;
            public mutable float nz;

            public constructor Vertex(float x, float y, float z, float nx, float ny, float nz) {
                this.x = x;
                this.y = y;
                this.z = z;
                this.nx = nx;
                this.ny = ny;
                this.nz = nz;
            }
        }
    }
}
